NEW FEATURE: ChangeGuard Code Health — check overall code quality, not only change impact:
SQL tuning, code-level performance, Java memory/GC practices, and coding guidelines.
Windows + Git Bash. Never print secret values. Do NOT change approval, executor, verdict or the existing
change-impact flow; all existing tests must keep passing. Read .bob/rules/changeguard.md, plans/CONTRACTS.md (v1.3),
services/changeguard-tools, services/backend and services/mission-control first.
Plan all phases now (files, functions, rules, tests, how each acceptance check is met), then implement ONE phase at a
time and stop after each phase for my review.

CORE RULES
- Deterministic checkers detect and measure; the LLM never decides whether something is an issue.
- Every finding has: id, category (sql | performance | memory | standards), rule_id, severity
  (critical | high | medium | low), file, line, snippet, message, recommendation, and evidence_label:
  "proven" (measured), "detected" (rule fired) or "opinion" (AI review). Only "proven" or critical "detected"
  findings can make a scan BLOCKED; "opinion" never blocks.
- Two scan modes: "change" (only files changed between base_ref and head_ref) and "full" (whole target).
- Baseline: a full scan can be saved as baseline; change scans report "new" vs "existing" per finding
  (match by rule_id + file + normalised snippet hash, not line number).
- Suppressions file .changeguard/suppressions.yaml (rule_id, file glob, reason, owner, expires); expired ones are ignored
  and reported.
- Rule packs are YAML in services/changeguard-tools/rules/health/ so the organisation can edit them without code changes.

PHASE 1 — Sample code + static checker engine (tools package)
1. Create samples/code-health/ (committed, outside fixture/): a small Java 17 service (5–8 classes) and SQL files with
   deliberate, realistic issues covering every rule below, plus a clean file with no issues. Add a README listing
   each planted issue with file:line (this is the answer key for tests).
2. services/changeguard-tools/changeguard_tools/health/: a rule engine (Python, no network) that scans .java, .sql,
   MyBatis XML and SQL strings in Java (@Query, JDBC execute/prepareStatement literals), using regex plus light
   parsing (comments and string literals handled; no matches inside comments).
3. Rule packs (YAML) with at least:
   SQL: SELECT *; UPDATE/DELETE without WHERE (critical); leading-wildcard LIKE; function on a column in WHERE;
        NOT IN subquery; OR chain on one column (suggest IN); ORDER BY without LIMIT on list queries;
        query built by string concatenation (also security).
   Performance: DB/HTTP call inside a loop (N+1); string concatenation with + in a loop; Pattern.compile or
        new ObjectMapper inside a loop/method body called per request; nested loops over collections with contains();
        HTTP/DB client without timeout; logging string concatenation without guard.
   Memory/GC: System.gc(); finalize(); streams/connections/statements not in try-with-resources; static mutable
        collections (static List/Map with add/put); ThreadLocal without remove(); unbounded caches (static HashMap used
        as cache); boxing in hot loops (Integer/Long accumulation).
   Standards: empty catch; catch (Exception|Throwable) that swallows; printStackTrace; System.out in non-test code;
        hard-coded credentials/URLs; method longer than 60 lines; more than 5 parameters; magic numbers in conditions.
4. Optional external tools: if `java` and PMD are installed, also run PMD and merge its findings (label "detected");
   if not, skip and say so in the result. sqlfluff (pip, pure Python) for SQL style if installed; skip if not.
5. Tests: every planted issue in samples/code-health is found (by rule_id and line), the clean file has zero findings,
   comments don't trigger rules, suppressions and expiry work, baseline new/existing matching works.
   Report precision/recall against the README answer key.

PHASE 2 — SQL proof with SQLite (stdlib, no install)
1. For each SQL query in samples/code-health that targets the sample schema, load the schema into an in-memory
   SQLite database seeded with realistic volume (e.g. 50 000 orders, 5 000 customers) and run EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN.
2. If the plan shows a full table SCAN on a filtered/joined table, attach the plan as evidence and set the finding
   to "proven" ("full scan of orders (50 000 rows) for WHERE customer_id = ?").
3. Propose the fix (index or query rewrite), apply it to a COPY of the in-memory database, re-run EXPLAIN, and attach
   the before/after plans and timings (median of 5 runs). Never touch a real database.
4. Clearly state in every SQL proof: "SQLite test database, seeded data; production engine plans may differ."
5. Tests for proven/not-proven cases and the disclaimer.

